 really visible also because within each  block sharp edges are being approximated  by some of the functions cosines you  might see halos or oscillations around  sharp edges another way to cause  glitching in a JPEG is using the  original source of a lot of glitching  that is data corruption what happens  when we manually curve up to the data of  a JPEG file well if we're doing it  manually  we want to be careful to specifically  avoid touching the header information or  any other information that will mean  that the file is not recognized as a  JPEG anymore so we only want to modify  the compressed image data itself each of  these sections has a marker according to  the JPEG specification so we can scan  the image file for the marker for that  section and only modify the data between  it and the end of the image file  incidentally the marker for this area is  FF da we can convert the image data to  byte data readable as hex and at a  random location in the compressed image  data we can replace a single byte with  another randomly generated byte and we  can repeat this for as many glitches as  we like because the speck of a jpg  renderer only tells a renderer what to  do with correct data renderers each have  different ways of handling what data is  incorrect so we have modified exactly  one byte in each of these images and we  can see that a JPEG with one randomly  corrupted by it looks different in  Safari where the error stops after one  row and chrome where that error travels  through the rest of the image so to save  space the constant component of each  block in a JPEG that is the coefficient  of the zero zero basis function for that  block is based on the difference between  it in the previous blocks so chrome  might be carrying that error forward in  Safari the error stops after one row to  render the  maybe safari just starts from scratch  and doesn't look at the previous row  how can modifying a single bite  sometimes result in a color change well  when we modify a random byte of data in  the image data since color channel data  is separate for each block we might only  affect one of the color channels leading  to a completely different color and in  the case of Chrome it would continue for  the rest of the image  how does modifying a single byte  sometimes result in a position shift  well each 8 by 8 block of JPEG data  contains information about its overall  position in the image and if we modify a  random byte of the image data we might  affect the location information and  cause the JPEG renderer to draw that  block in a different place so after  learning about all these ways to glitch  data JPEG data specifically and played  around with them I settled on a  combination of artifacting and random  manual data corruption of compressed  image information so now I'll show a  demo in a couple of different browsers  and renderer since it'll look different  image  okay so this example is with chrome this  is me and my cut so first I will  artifact it and then apply the glitch to  it also super interesting thing is the  OS preview renderer is different so if  you open it up it'll look like this so  you can like actually see all the basis  functions in here which is pretty cool  so yeah it's completely different  behavior and each  okay so if you would like to play with  those glitches yourself you can find  them at sailor HT comm slash glitchy or  sailor HD comm slash the G / - dot HTML  for the JPEG specific ones and they  don't currently work very well on mobile  sorry so you'll have to look at them on  a desktop but yeah thanks for having me  and come talk to me if you want to talk  about images or glitching or computer  science education thank you  [Applause]
 

